  "reading": "The candidate-count exponent b moves further under a change of ESTIMATOR (raw-space Gauss-Newton NLS vs ordinary log-space OLS, same functional form, same 20 cells) than it does under deleting any single K column, and the log-OLS value falls outside the column-deletion range the paper quotes. The operating-point coefficient c is by contrast stable under both estimators and all deletions. This is a fitted-parameter stability caveat on b, not a validity failure of the surface: the two estimators minimize absolute and relative error respectively, and on a grid whose cell means span roughly two orders of magnitude they weight it very differently. Raw-space NLS remains the reported fit because its R^2 is then commensurable with the additive model's at equal parameter count. b is not a quantity this paper's conclusions rest on; c is, and c survives."
